  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Platizio NextGen\n\nI read the actual source (not just the article-content excerpts) — `IntroProvider`, `Preloader`, `SmoothScroll`, `Reveal`, `MarketConstellation`, `Nav`, `Products`/`ProductsIndex`, `ArticlePage`/`LegalPage`, `AboutPage`, and the `app/*/page.tsx` route files — plus `next.config.ts`/`package.json`. Key context: this is a **fully static marketing site** (Next.js 16 / React 19, `generateStaticParams` + `dynamicParams = false` on every dynamic route, zero `fetch`/`axios`/ORM calls anywhere in `src`). That materially changes which of your five categories actually apply.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — **none exist, and can't**\nThere is no data-fetching layer. `src/lib/articles.ts`, `products.ts`, `legal.ts` are all in-memory object/array registries baked into the bundle at build time via SSG. I confirmed with a repo-wide grep for `fetch(`, `axios`, `prisma`, `supabase`, `useSWR`/`useQuery` — zero matches. Nothing to fix here; flagging so you don't go looking for a problem that isn't there.\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders — 1 real bug, 1 real duplication pattern\n\n**Bug: the preloader silently restarts and re-locks scroll after the intro finishes.**\n`src/components/IntroProvider.tsx:17`\n```tsx\n<Preloader onDone={() => setDone(true)} />\n```\nThis inline arrow gets a new identity on every `IntroProvider` render. `Preloader`'s animation-loop effect depends on it:\n```tsx\n// src/components/Preloader.tsx:61\n}, [reduce, onDone]);\n```\nTrace: when the curtain finishes, the effect's `setTimeout` calls `onDone()` → `setDone(true)` in the parent → `IntroProvider` re-renders → **a brand-new `onDone` closure** is passed to `Preloader` → its effect's dependency changed → React tears down and **re-runs the whole effect**: `document.documentElement.style.overflow = \"hidden\"` fires again and a fresh `requestAnimationFrame` tick loop restarts the 0→100 progress animation for another ~1.78s (`LOAD_MS` + `HOLD_MS`). `doneRef.current` stops `onDone()` from firing twice, but nothing stops the scroll-lock and RAF churn from repeating. Net effect: right after the curtain visually lifts, the page becomes **unscrollable again for ~1.8 seconds** and burns another ~100 wasted state updates — invisible in casual testing because nothing renders (`visible` is already `false`), but very much there.\n\nFix — make the callback identity stable:\n```tsx\n// IntroProvider.tsx\nconst [done, setDone] = useState(false);\nconst handleDone = useCallback(() => setDone(true), []);\nreturn (\n  <IntroContext.Provider value={done}>\n    <Preloader onDone={handleDone} />\n    {children}\n  </IntroContext.Provider>\n);\n```\n\n**Duplicated hover-state re-render pattern (2 places).** `src/components/sections/Products.tsx` and `src/components/pages/ProductsIndex.tsx` both do:\n```tsx\nconst activate = (i: number) => { setActiveIndex(i); setPreviewIndex(i); };\n...\n{PRODUCTS.map((product, i) => (\n  <ProductRow onActivate={() => activate(i)} onDeactivate={() => setActiveIndex(null)} ... />\n))}\n```\nEvery row gets a fresh inline handler on every render, and `ProductRow` isn't `memo`'d, so hovering one row re-renders the entire list (5 rows) plus the sticky preview panel. At 5 items this is harmless, but it's the textbook anti-pattern and it's copy-pasted in two files — worth fixing once and sharing the component. Either wrap `ProductRow` in `React.memo` + `useCallback` the handlers, or better, drop the JS hover state entirely and use CSS `:hover`/`:focus-within` (nothing here needs to synchronize with the sticky preview except the preview swap itself, which could stay on a lighter `onPointerEnter` without touching two pieces of state per row).\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\nGiven the static-content architecture, there's little missing here — images already use `next/image` with `fill`/`sizes` correctly (`AboutPage.tsx`), and pages are pre-rendered at build time. The one gap: `readingMinutes()`/`buildToc()` (see #5) are pure functions of static per-page data but are recomputed on every client re-render instead of once — see below.\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — **none found**\nI checked every `useEffect` that adds a listener or timer (`SmoothScroll`, `Preloader`, `Nav`'s scroll/overflow effects, `MarketConstellation`'s `pointermove` listener and `geometry.dispose()`, the `subscribeReducedMotion` `useSyncExternalStore`). All of them return proper cleanup — RAF cancellation, listener removal, geometry disposal. This is a genuinely disciplined codebase on that front; I'm not going to manufacture a leak that isn't there. The closest thing to a leak-shaped problem is the effect-restart bug above, but memory doesn't grow — it's wasted CPU/UX, not a leak.\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n**`MarketConstellation.tsx` — full terrain recompute every frame, forever, regardless of scroll position.** `src/components/three/MarketConstellation.tsx:34-83`: `Terrain` runs `useFrame` with `frameloop=\"always\"` (line 182), recomputing `waveY()` (3 trig calls) for all `COLS*ROWS = 6,050` points and re-uploading the buffer, at 60fps, for as long as the homepage is mounted — even after the user has scrolled far past the hero into `Ticker`/`Manifesto`/`Products`/etc. There's no `IntersectionObserver`-based pause. Fix: gate `frameloop` on hero visibility, e.g.\n```tsx\nconst [inView, setInView] = useState(true);\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const el = canvasWrapperRef.current;\n  const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => setInView(e.isIntersecting), { threshold: 0 });\n  io.observe(el);\n  return () => io.disconnect();\n}, []);\n// <Canvas frameloop={reduced || !inView ? \"demand\" : \"always\"} ...>\n```\n\n**`ArticlePage.tsx` / `LegalPage.tsx` — pure content functions recomputed on every render instead of memoized once.**\n```tsx\n// ArticlePage.tsx:24-30\nconst toc = buildToc(body);              // walks every block\nconst minutes = readingMinutes(body);    // splits every block's text on whitespace\nconst related = ARTICLES_SORTED.filter(...).slice(0, 2);\n```\n`body`/`article` are stable for the component's lifetime (both files are `\"use client\"`, and `body`/`doc.blocks` never change after mount), but none of this is wrapped in `useMemo`. On a long legal document (`privacy-policy.ts` is thousands of words) this walks the whole block tree again on any re-render triggered upstream (e.g. the `useReducedMotion()` media-query listener firing). Low severity given render frequency here, but a one-line fix:\n```tsx\nconst toc = useMemo(() => buildToc(body), [body]);\nconst minutes = useMemo(() => readingMinutes(body), [body]);\nconst related = useMemo(\n  () => ARTICLES_SORTED.filter(a => a.slug !== article.slug && a.category === article.category).slice(0, 2),\n  [article.slug, article.category],\n);\n```\n\n**`AboutPage.tsx` pays a redundant ~1.8s preloader tax that every other interior page explicitly opts out of.** `src/components/site/SiteShell.tsx`'s own comment says interior pages \"skip the drenched preloader so navigation stays instant,\" relying on `IntroContext`'s default value of `true`. But `AboutPage.tsx:406-413` wraps itself in its own `<IntroProvider><SmoothScroll>` instead of using `SiteShell`, so navigating to `/about` re-triggers the full curtain animation (RAF tick loop + scroll lock) that `/products`, `/insights`, `/contact`, and the legal pages don't pay. This looks like an oversight rather than intent — worth switching `AboutPage` to `SiteShell` like its siblings, both for consistency and to drop the redundant animation work.\n\n### Priority order\n1. Fix the `IntroProvider`/`Preloader` callback-identity bug (#2) — real user-facing bug (scroll lock returns after intro), one-line `useCallback` fix.\n2. Gate `MarketConstellation`'s `frameloop` on viewport visibility (#5) — biggest ongoing CPU cost.\n3. Switch `AboutPage` to `SiteShell` to drop the duplicate preloader (#5).\n4. `useMemo` the `toc`/`minutes`/`related` derivations in `ArticlePage`/`LegalPage` (#5) — cheap, low-risk cleanup.\n5. Dedupe the `Products`/`ProductsIndex` hover pattern if you touch that code again (#2) — not urgent at 5 items.\n",
